 "<code data-rich-format=\"{&quot;annotations&quot;:{&quot;1361&quot;:{&quot;binding&quot;:&quot;var-_uniq.669&quot;,&quot;cssClass&quot;:&quot;var&quot;},&quot;2&quot;:{&quot;binding&quot;:&quot;var-_uniq.668&quot;,&quot;cssClass&quot;:&quot;var&quot;},&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;binding&quot;:&quot;var-_uniq.669&quot;,&quot;cssClass&quot;:&quot;var&quot;},&quot;341&quot;:{&quot;binding&quot;:&quot;var-_uniq.669&quot;,&quot;cssClass&quot;:&quot;var&quot;},&quot;84&quot;:{&quot;binding&quot;:&quot;var-_uniq.668&quot;,&quot;cssClass&quot;:&quot;var&quot;}},&quot;fmt&quot;:[6,[6,[3,2,[4,[6,[3,2,[4,[6,[3,2,&quot;rfl&quot;]],[4,&quot;.{&quot;,[4,[6,[3,2,&quot;u&quot;]],&quot;}&quot;]]]]],[4,1,[4,[6,[3,2,[4,&quot;{&quot;,[4,[7,2,&quot;α&quot;],[4,&quot; :&quot;,[4,1,[4,[6,[3,2,[4,&quot;Sort&quot;,[4,1,[6,[3,2,&quot;u&quot;]]]]]],&quot;}&quot;]]]]]]],[4,1,[4,[6,[3,2,[4,&quot;{&quot;,[4,[7,3,&quot;a&quot;],[4,&quot; :&quot;,[4,1,[4,[6,[3,2,[7,84,&quot;α&quot;]]],&quot;}&quot;]]]]]]],[4,&quot; :&quot;,[4,1,[6,[3,2,[4,[6,[3,2,[7,1361,&quot;a&quot;]]],[4,[4,&quot; =&quot;,1],[6,[3,2,[7,341,&quot;a&quot;]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]}\">rfl.{u} {α : Sort u} {a : α} : a = a</code><span class=\"sep\"></span><code class=\"docstring\">`rfl : a = a` is the unique constructor of the equality type. This is the\nsame as `Eq.refl` except that it takes `a` implicitly instead of explicitly.\n\nThis is a more powerful theorem than it may appear at first, because although\nthe statement of the theorem is `a = a`, Lean will allow anything that is\ndefinitionally equal to that type. So, for instance, `2 + 2 = 4` is proven in\nLean by `rfl`, because both sides are the same up to definitional equality.\n</code>",
